Funding-Acknowledgement = {Municipality of Santa Cruz; Charles Darwin Foundation for the Galpagos
   Islands {[}PC-10-23, PC-10-24]},
Funding-Text = {We thank the Galapagos National Park Directorate (GNPD) for their
   continued collaboration, provision of endemic plant seedlings, and
   support in community-based restoration efforts. We acknowledge the
   Biosecurity Agency for the Galapagos (ABG) for their ongoing assistance
   with quarantine processes, and for their active role in implementing
   ecological gardens throughout Puerto Ayora with the DPNG. Our
   appreciation extends to the Municipality of Santa Cruz for providing
   essential spatial data and promoting native flora conservation,
   particularly by designating Scalesia affinis as the emblematic plant of
   Puerto Ayora. We are also grateful to the Herbarium of the Charles
   Darwin Foundation for granting access to its plant collections,
   essential to the species distribution analysis, and to Paul Mayorga,
   Anna Calle-Loor, and Thijs Strik for fieldwork and data collection
   support. This publication is contribution No. 2722 of the Charles Darwin
   Foundation for the Galapagos Islands and was conducted under Research
   Permits No. PC-10-23 and PC-10-24. We also thank the four anonymous
